 have some ambiguity to them or some  layering to them narratively  so that if you see them a second time  you're going to watch them in slightly  different way  and that was that was my approach there  are other filmmakers who approached it  from a purely  visual point of view where they would  just create very dense visuals that  would sustain multiple viewings because  you'd sort of see more in the frame  and so the structure i settled on is one  that  it's based on a musical structure called  the shepherd tone which is a  musical illusion whereby you can keep  climbing up the scale  it's something i'd used in my schools  like the david julian school for the  prestige was the first time we  experimented with it  so you're continually going up the scale  going up the scale but you never seem to  go  out of out of reach if you like and i  wanted to try and apply that  to screenwriting to a narrative and say  okay with this story  can you braid together the three  storylines in such a way that  you create the idea of a continuing rise  in  intensity narratively  and sustaining that is the challenge  of the structure and the way in which i  approach  different or complex structures as i  have in the past  um and and i do with this film is you  diagram it you you get a hold of it  mathematically so you know exactly what  it's meant to be doing  but then you sit down and you write the  script from page one right through to  the end of the film  the way the audience is going to be  receiving that information the hope is  that in that way  you're actually creating a cohesive and  organic rhythm and story movement  built on a very uh  constructed you know set of of  parameters but not through  cutting and pasting not creating a  fractured narrative so much as  disparate story threads that braid  together to make one you know coherent  story  i'm a self-taught filmmaker i never went  to film school i never  studied filmmaking in any way i started  making films  uh when i w